part.text:
**FOUND IT!** In Spring Security 5.7.3, `@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity` is meta-annotated with **`@Configuration`** (see constant #32 = Lorg/springframework/context/annotation/Configuration; and RuntimeVisibleAnnotations entry `5: #32()` = @Configuration)! So in SS 5.7, a class annotated with @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity became a full @Configuration-lite (lite mode, since @Configuration without proxyBeanMethods → lite) → component scanning... wait, @Configuration meta-annotation makes the class eligible for component scanning? Hmm — @Configuration is meta-annotated with @Component, so a class with @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity (which carries @Configuration → @Component) IS picked up by component scanning AND processed as a "lite" configuration class with @Bean methods! That's how 7.9.3 worked!

In Spring Security **6.x**, `@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity` was REMOVED (deprecated in 5.8, removed in 6.0)! I replaced it with `@EnableMethodSecurity` — which in SS 6.1 is NOT meta-annotated with @Configuration (let me verify... the v8 template explicitly adds @Configuration to the class!). Look at the v8 template:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity(securedEnabled = true)
public class SecurityConfiguration {
```

YES — v8 has @Configuration! So the fix: add @Configuration to my SecurityConfiguration. That makes it a full configuration class, @Bean methods registered, constructor injection works.

