    }
    ...
    return null;  // ABSTAIN → hmm — what does AuthorizationFilter do with null decision?
}
```

AuthorizationFilter.doFilterInternal → `this.authorizationManager.check(...)` → if decision == null → `null` decision → hmm — AuthorizationFilter: 

```java
AuthorizationDecision decision = this.authorizationManager.check(this::getAuthentication, request);
if (decision != null && !decision.isGranted()) { throw new AccessDeniedException(...) }
```

**A NULL decision means GRANTED!** So if RequestMatcherDelegatingAuthorizationManager returns null for unmatched → request is permitted!!

Hmm!! But my empirical test showed 401 on / (unauthenticated GET /) — so unmatched was DENIED in my app. Why? Because — AH: `AuthorizeHttpRequestsConfigurer` adds a DEFAULT trailing mapping! Let me check the SS 6.1.5 source of AuthorizeHttpRequestsConfigurer... I recall:

```java
